course: money
lesson: 36
title: Taxes and the government's budget
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Taxes are money that people and businesses must pay to the government, and they pay for things everybody shares: schools, hospitals, roads, bins and police.
explain: Picture your classroom: it was reported that each pupil in a state school costs about £7,000 a year, so a class of 30 costs about 30 × £7,000 = £210,000, and that money comes from everyone, through taxes.
explain: Some taxes are taken from wages, called income tax; some hide inside prices, like VAT, which is £2 of a £12 toy in the UK; and council tax pays for local things like potholes and bins.
explain: Each year the government plans its spending in a budget, and in the UK the minister in charge holds up a famous red box on budget day; if it spends more than it collects, it borrows, and its debt grows.
explain: People argue hard about tax: some say "cut taxes, let people keep their money", others say "raise taxes, our hospitals need it"; both sides have reasons, and voters decide which way to go.
fact: Taxes are money paid to the government; public services are services for everyone, like schools, hospitals, roads and police.
fact: It was reported that a state school pupil costs roughly £7,000 a year, so a class of 30 costs about 30 × £7,000 = £210,000.
fact: VAT, or value added tax, is a tax inside many prices; in the UK it adds £2 to every £10, so a £12 toy is £10 for the shop and £2 for the government, while food like bread and children's clothes often have none.
fact: Income tax is tax on what you earn; some money is tax-free, and on earnings above that the basic rate takes £1 of every £5, written 20%, so £200 of £1,000, because £1,000 ÷ 5 = £200.
fact: The government's budget is its plan for money; a deficit is spending more than it collects, like spending £100 and collecting £90, so it borrows £10.
fact: A tax cut leaves more money in people's pockets but less for public services; a tax rise does the opposite; people disagree about which is better.
fact: In the UK the NHS, the National Health Service, is paid for by taxes, so an X-ray and a cast cost £0 at the hospital even though they cost hundreds of pounds to provide; other countries have different systems.
fact: Councils use taxes to fix roads but money is limited; at about £75 a pothole, 100 potholes cost 100 × £75 = £7,500, so they must choose what comes first.
fact: People who earn more usually pay more tax in pounds and often a bigger share; someone on £20,000 might pay £2,000 and someone on £100,000 about £30,000.
fact: A progressive tax is one where richer people pay a bigger share; some say they should pay even more, others say it is already a lot.
fact: Paying tax is a legal duty; tax evasion, illegally not paying, can mean fines or prison; if 1 in 10 people didn't pay, about 1 in every 10 pounds of tax would be missing.
fact: The national debt is all the money a government has borrowed and still owes, and it must pay interest on it, just like a family with a loan.
fact: 1 trillion is 1,000 billion, which is 1,000,000 million.
exercise: Who pays for a state school? | Everyone, through taxes | The head teacher, out of her own pay | The children, from their pocket money | Nobody, schools are free to run
exercise: A pupil costs about £7,000 a year. What does a class of 30 cost? | £210,000 | £21,000 | £7,030 | £2,100,000
exercise: What is VAT? | A tax hidden inside the price of many things you buy | A tax that is only paid by people who own cars or vans | A fee for using a cash machine | A discount given to children in shops
exercise: About how much of a £12 toy is VAT in the UK? | About £2 | About £12 | About £1.20 | About 20p
exercise: Ella's mum pays income tax of £1 in every £5 on £1,000 above the tax-free amount. How much tax is that? | £200 | £20 | £100 | £800
exercise: What is income tax? | Tax taken from what people earn | Tax on things that people buy in shops | Tax that is paid only by big businesses | Tax paid on houses and flats each month
exercise: The minister holds up a red box on budget day. What's inside, as Dad says? | The plans for the country's money | The country's gold and jewels | Letters from children to the King | Every tax paid that year in cash
exercise: The government spends £100 and collects £90 in taxes. What must it do? | Borrow £10 | Save £10 | Borrow £190 | Nothing at all
exercise: Some people say "Cut taxes!" and others say "Raise taxes for hospitals!" What is true? | Both sides have reasons, and people disagree | Only the people who want cuts have any reasons | Only the people who want rises have any reasons | Nobody ever really argues about taxes at all
exercise: Zara gets an X-ray and a cast at an NHS hospital. What does her family pay at the hospital? | £0, because it is paid for by taxes | Hundreds of pounds, paid in cash | £10, the price of a plaster | A fee for every hour in the waiting room
exercise: A pothole costs about £75 to fix. How much do 100 potholes cost? | £7,500 | £750 | £175 | £75,000
exercise: Why do councils take a long time to fix every pothole? | Money is limited, so they must choose what comes first | Councils are not allowed by law to fix roads in winter | Potholes fix themselves if you wait | Council workers only work in summer
exercise: Ruby asks if rich people pay more tax. What did Dad say? | Usually more pounds, and whether it's enough is argued about | No, rich people pay no tax at all | Yes, they pay all of the tax in the country and nobody else pays any | Rich and poor always pay exactly the same pounds
exercise: Someone on £100,000 pays about £30,000 and someone on £20,000 pays £2,000. How much more does the first pay? | £28,000 | £32,000 | £80,000 | £10,000
exercise: What would happen if lots of people refused to pay tax? | Less money for schools, bins and police, and it's against the law | Everyone would get richer with nothing lost | The government would pay everyone instead | Nothing, tax is just a suggestion
exercise: Leo hears "The government owes trillions." How many billions are in 1 trillion? | 1,000 | 100 | 1,000,000 | 10
exercise: Who does the government borrow from? | People, banks and other countries, who lend it money | Only from the money in children's savings accounts | Only from the King's own purse, which never runs out | Nobody lends; it just takes the money it needs from shops
test: Your mate goes, "School's free, nobody pays for it, innit." What's the truth? | Everyone pays through taxes, about £7,000 a pupil a year | He's right, schools cost nothing to run | Schools are paid for by the teachers out of their own wages | Only parents with children at school pay
test: Your cousin from Texas says, "Y'all don't pay for the doctor? That's wild, we got bills." ("Y'all" means you all.) What's true? | In the UK the NHS is paid by taxes; other countries have different systems | Healthcare is free everywhere in the world | Healthcare costs the same in every country | Americans never pay anything for doctors
test: A neighbour moans, "Took 'em 2 years to fix that pothole, proper useless." What might explain it? | Councils have limited money and have to choose which roads come first | Councils are not allowed to fix roads at all | Potholes are fixed only by volunteers | The pothole was protected by a special law for two whole years, so it stayed
test: Dad buys a £10 toy and Jack goes, "VAT? Sounds sus." ("Sus" means dodgy.) What's the honest answer? | It's a normal tax inside many prices that goes to the government | It's a secret extra fee that the shop keeps for itself as profit | It's a fine for buying toys on Saturday | It's a discount the shop forgot to give
test: What would happen if a government spent £100 every year but collected only £90? | It would borrow £10 a year, and its debt would grow | It would save £10 a year by putting it in a big piggy bank | Nothing, spending more than you collect is completely free | Its debt would shrink by £10 a year until it was all gone
test: A Glaswegian granny says, "Cut the taxes? Aye, and who's payin' for ma hip at the hospital, hen?" ("Hen" means dear.) A shop owner says, "Lower tax, I could hire another worker." What's going on? | Both have real reasons; people disagree about taxes | The granny is right and the shop owner is lying | The shop owner is right, and nobody really needs hospitals any more | Neither is talking about tax at all
test: Ella's mum earns £1,000 above the tax-free amount — sorry, £2,000. At £1 in every £5, how much tax? | £400 | £200 | £2,000 | £20
test: In a cartoon, a villain refuses to pay tax and the town gets new schools anyway. What's wrong? | Taxes pay for schools; if people don't pay, there's less money for them | Nothing, schools appear without any money | Villains pay all the taxes in real towns | Schools are paid by fines on cartoons
test: Your mate says, "Rich people pay the same tax as everyone, it's a flat 20 quid." What's closer to the truth? | People who earn more usually pay more pounds, and often a bigger share | Everyone pays exactly £20 a year, rich or poor | Rich people pay no tax because they're rich | Only children pay tax in the UK
test: Leo's mate says, "A trillion is just a million with extra zeros, like 10 million." What is 1 trillion? | 1,000 billion, which is 1,000,000 million | 10 million | 1,000 million | 100 billion
test: The council has £7,500 for potholes this year. Each fix costs £75. A Gen Z neighbour says, "So like, how many is that, it's giving not enough." ("It's giving" means it looks like.) How many can be fixed? | 100 | 75 | 1,000 | 10
exam: It is given that the cost of educating one pupil is £7,000 per annum. Determine the cost of educating a class of thirty pupils. | £210,000 | £21,000 | £7,030 | £2,100,000
exam: Ascertain the income tax payable at a rate of one pound in every five upon earnings of £1,000 in excess of the tax-free allowance. | £200 | £20 | £100 | £800
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a budget deficit? | An excess of government expenditure over the revenue collected in taxation | An excess of revenue collected in taxation over government expenditure | A tax levied upon goods purchased in shops | A sum paid to the government by children
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning public opinion upon the level of taxation. | Opinion is divided, each side advancing reasons for its position | All persons agree that taxation should be abolished | All persons agree that taxation should be doubled | The level of taxation is not a matter of any public interest or debate
